1
00:00:00,065 --> 00:00:01,589
November 2008.

2
00:00:02,005 --> 00:00:04,036
A doctor from Chicago checks into the

3
00:00:04,036 --> 00:00:06,549
Taj Mahal Palace Hotel in Mumbai.

4
00:00:06,966 --> 00:00:09,701
He is there on business — his immigration

5
00:00:09,714 --> 00:00:12,031
consultancy has an office in the city.

6
00:00:13,299 --> 00:00:16,099
About eleven days later, gunmen will be

7
00:00:16,112 --> 00:00:17,804
walking the same corridors.

8
00:00:18,978 --> 00:00:20,462
He never fires a shot.

9
00:00:20,866 --> 00:00:22,494
He never plans a target.

10
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:25,788
What he supplies is rarer: a cover story.

11
00:00:26,963 --> 00:00:29,750
Three years later, an American jury clears

12
00:00:29,763 --> 00:00:32,145
him of helping plan the Mumbai attacks.

13
00:00:33,401 --> 00:00:33,896
Cleared.

14
00:00:34,313 --> 00:00:36,943
And fourteen years after that, India flies

15
00:00:36,956 --> 00:00:38,896
him to Delhi to stand trial for them

16
00:00:38,909 --> 00:00:39,404
anyway.

17
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:42,125
How can a man be acquitted of an attack —

18
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:44,573
and still be sent home to answer for it?

19
00:00:51,957 --> 00:00:53,793
Everything here comes from the record —

20
00:00:53,806 --> 00:00:57,035
court filings, the NIA's  case, and

21
00:00:57,048 --> 00:00:58,910
PBS Frontline's reporting.

22
00:00:59,327 --> 00:01:01,514
And every one of those accounts starts in

23
00:01:01,527 --> 00:01:02,478
the same place.

24
00:01:03,708 --> 00:01:06,286
A military prep school in Punjab called

25
00:01:06,299 --> 00:01:08,226
Cadet College Hasan Abdal.

26
00:01:09,404 --> 00:01:11,787
Tahawwur Rana was the son of a school

27
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:12,451
principal.

28
00:01:12,827 --> 00:01:15,444
The other boy was born Daood Gilani.

29
00:01:15,849 --> 00:01:18,167
The world would later know him as David

30
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:19,053
Coleman Headley.

31
00:01:19,508 --> 00:01:22,126
He had an American mother and a Pakistani

32
00:01:22,138 --> 00:01:22,698
father.

33
00:01:23,102 --> 00:01:25,810
Classmates never forgot his eyes — two

34
00:01:25,823 --> 00:01:26,722
different colours.

35
00:01:27,888 --> 00:01:29,568
The two boys became close.

36
00:01:29,958 --> 00:01:31,742
Then their lives split apart.

37
00:01:32,118 --> 00:01:34,045
Rana went into medicine.

38
00:01:34,477 --> 00:01:36,560
Headley drifted into drug trafficking,

39
00:01:36,872 --> 00:01:39,151
then a stint as an informant for the US

40
00:01:39,164 --> 00:01:41,365
Drug Enforcement Administration, and

41
00:01:41,378 --> 00:01:43,214
eventually into radicalisation.

42
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,485
The friendship survived all of it.

43
00:01:46,902 --> 00:01:48,673
Rana's wife, Samraz,

44
00:01:49,285 --> 00:01:51,316
later told Frontline that the family

45
00:01:51,329 --> 00:01:53,178
considered Headley one of their own.

46
00:01:53,699 --> 00:01:55,118
He called her "sister."

47
00:01:55,587 --> 00:01:58,217
Her children called him "special uncle."

48
00:01:59,475 --> 00:02:01,311
That trust is the whole story.

49
00:02:01,728 --> 00:02:03,850
Everything that follows runs through it.

50
00:02:05,257 --> 00:02:07,796
On paper, Rana's life looked nothing like

51
00:02:07,809 --> 00:02:08,603
a terrorist's.

52
00:02:09,059 --> 00:02:11,025
He came from a respectable family:

53
00:02:11,742 --> 00:02:13,929
one brother became a psychiatrist in the

54
00:02:13,929 --> 00:02:16,911
Pakistan military, another a journalist in

55
00:02:16,924 --> 00:02:17,445
Canada.

56
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:19,424
He trained as a doctor.

57
00:02:19,854 --> 00:02:22,406
He served as a Captain in the Pakistan

58
00:02:22,406 --> 00:02:23,617
Army Medical Corps.

59
00:02:24,033 --> 00:02:26,937
Then, in the late 1990s, he deserted.

60
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,069
The reasons are still unclear.

61
00:02:31,237 --> 00:02:34,284
In 1997 he emigrated to Canada with his

62
00:02:34,297 --> 00:02:36,133
wife, also a physician.

63
00:02:36,563 --> 00:02:39,323
Records show both became Canadian citizens

64
00:02:39,336 --> 00:02:40,404
in 2001.

65
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:43,255
From Toronto, he moved into business and

66
00:02:43,268 --> 00:02:44,531
settled in Chicago.

67
00:02:44,935 --> 00:02:46,784
There was a halal meat business on

68
00:02:46,797 --> 00:02:47,656
Devon Avenue.

69
00:02:48,073 --> 00:02:49,844
And there was First World Immigration

70
00:02:49,857 --> 00:02:52,669
Services — offices across North America

71
00:02:52,682 --> 00:02:55,052
and, eventually, a branch in Mumbai.

72
00:02:56,199 --> 00:02:58,595
It was a real business, with real clients.

73
00:02:59,868 --> 00:03:02,147
That is exactly what made it useful.

74
00:03:03,484 --> 00:03:06,401
According to Indian investigators, Headley

75
00:03:06,414 --> 00:03:09,161
used the Mumbai branch as his cover story.

76
00:03:09,605 --> 00:03:12,313
He travelled to India again and again as

77
00:03:12,326 --> 00:03:13,785
an immigration consultant.

78
00:03:14,201 --> 00:03:17,053
His real assignment, investigators say,

79
00:03:17,365 --> 00:03:19,800
came from Lashkar-e-Taiba's Hafiz Saeed

80
00:03:19,813 --> 00:03:22,495
and Zaki-ur-Rehman Lakhvi: surveil the

81
00:03:22,509 --> 00:03:23,238
targets.

82
00:03:24,445 --> 00:03:27,245
Rana made his own trip roughly eleven days

83
00:03:27,258 --> 00:03:28,312
before the attack.

84
00:03:28,768 --> 00:03:30,630
He stayed at the Taj — one of the

85
00:03:30,643 --> 00:03:32,948
buildings that would soon be under siege.

86
00:03:33,364 --> 00:03:36,294
NIA records place him on other trips under

87
00:03:36,307 --> 00:03:37,818
the same consultant cover.

88
00:03:39,063 --> 00:03:39,792
Agra.

89
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:40,938
Kochi.

90
00:03:41,316 --> 00:03:42,110
Hapur.

91
00:03:43,244 --> 00:03:45,184
So was he an unwitting friend?

92
00:03:45,602 --> 00:03:47,659
Or an informed participant?

93
00:03:48,932 --> 00:03:52,187
In early 2009, months after the attack,

94
00:03:52,396 --> 00:03:54,401
Headley phoned Rana in Chicago.

95
00:03:54,857 --> 00:03:57,213
He wanted to play him a recording — senior

96
00:03:57,213 --> 00:03:59,049
Lashkar commanders directing the ten

97
00:03:59,062 --> 00:04:00,351
gunmen in real time.

98
00:04:00,727 --> 00:04:02,706
One voice impressed Rana.

99
00:04:03,084 --> 00:04:05,896
It belonged to Sajid Mir, an alleged

100
00:04:05,909 --> 00:04:08,110
mastermind who remains at large.

101
00:04:08,529 --> 00:04:11,107
And Maharashtra's Anti-Terrorism Squad

102
00:04:11,641 --> 00:04:13,659
says it holds recordings of both men

103
00:04:13,672 --> 00:04:15,937
congratulating Lashkar's leadership on the

104
00:04:15,950 --> 00:04:17,890
"excellent planning and execution."

105
00:04:19,378 --> 00:04:21,917
Mumbai wasn't the only operation running

106
00:04:21,930 --> 00:04:22,998
through this friendship.

107
00:04:23,415 --> 00:04:25,784
The two men were also drawn into a plot

108
00:04:25,862 --> 00:04:27,438
against the Danish newspaper

109
00:04:27,451 --> 00:04:28,415
Jyllands-Posten,

110
00:04:29,053 --> 00:04:30,784
which had published cartoons of the

111
00:04:30,797 --> 00:04:32,907
Prophet Muhammad in 2005.

112
00:04:33,310 --> 00:04:35,771
Court documents describe a plan to attack

113
00:04:35,784 --> 00:04:36,813
the paper's staff.

114
00:04:37,954 --> 00:04:39,412
It was never carried out.

115
00:04:40,651 --> 00:04:42,057
Instead, it caught them.

116
00:04:42,475 --> 00:04:44,689
On October 18, 2009,

117
00:04:45,170 --> 00:04:47,866
federal agents arrested both men at O'Hare

118
00:04:47,879 --> 00:04:48,478
Airport.

119
00:04:49,707 --> 00:04:51,829
It was almost a year to the day after

120
00:04:51,829 --> 00:04:52,389
Mumbai.

121
00:04:53,791 --> 00:04:56,708
Headley pleaded guilty in March 2010.

122
00:04:57,151 --> 00:05:00,302
His deal brought 35 years in prison — and

123
00:05:00,315 --> 00:05:02,502
protection from extradition to India.

124
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:06,101
Then he took the stand against his oldest

125
00:05:06,114 --> 00:05:06,596
friend.

126
00:05:07,784 --> 00:05:10,519
Rana's trial opened in a Chicago federal

127
00:05:10,532 --> 00:05:13,136
court on May 16, 2011.

128
00:05:13,511 --> 00:05:15,256
It ran until June 9.

129
00:05:15,688 --> 00:05:18,266
His defence said he had been manipulated.

130
00:05:18,696 --> 00:05:20,870
He believed, his lawyers argued, that

131
00:05:20,883 --> 00:05:23,240
Headley's assignments came from Pakistan's

132
00:05:23,253 --> 00:05:26,104
intelligence service, the ISI — not a

133
00:05:26,118 --> 00:05:27,016
terrorist group.

134
00:05:27,433 --> 00:05:29,386
Prosecutors answered with emails,

135
00:05:29,998 --> 00:05:32,524
wiretaps and Rana's own words after his

136
00:05:32,537 --> 00:05:33,084
arrest.

137
00:05:34,245 --> 00:05:36,446
The jury split the verdict in two.

138
00:05:37,711 --> 00:05:39,742
Not guilty of supporting the Mumbai

139
00:05:39,742 --> 00:05:42,112
attacks — the event at the heart of this

140
00:05:42,125 --> 00:05:42,646
story.

141
00:05:43,063 --> 00:05:45,367
Guilty of supporting the Denmark plot,

142
00:05:45,823 --> 00:05:47,399
and of material support to

143
00:05:47,399 --> 00:05:48,571
Lashkar-e-Taiba.

144
00:05:48,987 --> 00:05:50,953
Judge Harry Leinenweber called it "a

145
00:05:50,966 --> 00:05:52,021
dastardly plot"

146
00:05:52,607 --> 00:05:54,469
and sentenced him to fourteen years,

147
00:05:54,977 --> 00:05:57,347
plus five years of supervised release.

148
00:05:58,510 --> 00:06:00,502
Not convinced enough for Mumbai.

149
00:06:00,919 --> 00:06:03,406
Convinced enough for everything around it.

150
00:06:04,675 --> 00:06:06,628
And that gap would decide the next

151
00:06:06,641 --> 00:06:07,566
fourteen years.

152
00:06:08,957 --> 00:06:10,129
Here is the hinge.

153
00:06:10,546 --> 00:06:13,202
In the US, Rana was tried for one specific

154
00:06:13,215 --> 00:06:15,585
crime connected to Mumbai — and acquitted.

155
00:06:15,988 --> 00:06:17,981
But India's charges were different.

156
00:06:18,358 --> 00:06:18,865
Murder.

157
00:06:19,283 --> 00:06:21,197
Waging war against the state.

158
00:06:21,587 --> 00:06:23,501
Terrorism under Indian law.

159
00:06:24,755 --> 00:06:25,680
Same events.

160
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:27,125
Different crimes.

161
00:06:28,313 --> 00:06:31,126
India had issued a warrant in 2018.

162
00:06:31,516 --> 00:06:33,001
Then came an odd twist.

163
00:06:33,417 --> 00:06:36,620
In 2020, Rana was released early from a US

164
00:06:36,633 --> 00:06:38,652
federal prison on COVID grounds.

165
00:06:39,924 --> 00:06:41,200
He was free for days.

166
00:06:41,617 --> 00:06:44,156
He was re-arrested — this time on India's

167
00:06:44,169 --> 00:06:45,406
extradition request,

168
00:06:45,862 --> 00:06:47,398
filed formally that June.

169
00:06:47,815 --> 00:06:50,172
Rana fought it for nearly five years.

170
00:06:50,562 --> 00:06:53,179
A magistrate certified the extradition.

171
00:06:53,556 --> 00:06:55,353
An appeals court upheld it.

172
00:06:55,757 --> 00:06:57,099
He took it all the way to the

173
00:06:57,112 --> 00:06:58,101
Supreme Court.

174
00:06:58,531 --> 00:07:00,731
His last argument was that he would face

175
00:07:00,744 --> 00:07:03,440
torture in India, in breach of the UN

176
00:07:03,453 --> 00:07:04,885
Convention Against Torture.

177
00:07:06,137 --> 00:07:07,075
It didn't work.

178
00:07:08,736 --> 00:07:10,989
The Ninth Circuit had already answered the

179
00:07:11,002 --> 00:07:11,848
core question.

180
00:07:12,252 --> 00:07:13,775
The crimes charged in India,

181
00:07:14,361 --> 00:07:15,077
the court wrote,

182
00:07:15,533 --> 00:07:18,085
have "elements independent" from those he

183
00:07:18,098 --> 00:07:19,387
had faced in America.

184
00:07:20,674 --> 00:07:23,526
Justice Elena Kagan turned down his final

185
00:07:23,539 --> 00:07:26,586
emergency application in March 2025.

186
00:07:26,976 --> 00:07:29,385
In April, the Court dismissed his last

187
00:07:29,398 --> 00:07:29,997
petition.

188
00:07:31,264 --> 00:07:32,748
No appeals were left.

189
00:07:33,139 --> 00:07:33,764
Not one.

190
00:07:35,157 --> 00:07:38,490
On April 9, 2025, US Marshals

191
00:07:38,504 --> 00:07:40,470
handed Rana to India's National

192
00:07:40,483 --> 00:07:41,863
Investigation Agency.

193
00:07:42,239 --> 00:07:44,440
A special flight carried him from Los

194
00:07:44,453 --> 00:07:45,169
Angeles.

195
00:07:46,445 --> 00:07:49,101
The next evening he landed in Delhi — and

196
00:07:49,114 --> 00:07:51,068
was arrested the moment he stepped off the

197
00:07:51,081 --> 00:07:51,549
plane.

198
00:07:52,737 --> 00:07:55,289
Secretary of State Marco Rubio said Rana

199
00:07:55,289 --> 00:07:57,425
would face justice for his role in the

200
00:07:57,425 --> 00:07:58,466
Mumbai attacks.

201
00:07:58,883 --> 00:08:01,500
For India, it closed a chase that began

202
00:08:01,513 --> 00:08:03,297
with the 2011 verdict.

203
00:08:04,564 --> 00:08:06,726
The trial it had wanted all along could

204
00:08:06,739 --> 00:08:07,754
finally begin.

205
00:08:09,116 --> 00:08:11,460
Rana is held in Tihar Jail.

206
00:08:11,878 --> 00:08:13,962
In the first weeks, Mumbai Police and the

207
00:08:13,975 --> 00:08:16,501
NIA reportedly questioned him for eight to

208
00:08:16,514 --> 00:08:17,504
ten hours a day.

209
00:08:17,907 --> 00:08:20,837
Reports described his answers as evasive.

210
00:08:21,254 --> 00:08:23,623
Investigators took voice and handwriting

211
00:08:23,636 --> 00:08:26,280
samples, to match against intercepted

212
00:08:26,293 --> 00:08:27,790
communications with Headley.

213
00:08:28,194 --> 00:08:30,472
Solicitor General Tushar Mehta was

214
00:08:30,485 --> 00:08:32,907
appointed to lead the prosecution himself.

215
00:08:34,097 --> 00:08:36,311
A supplementary chargesheet followed in

216
00:08:36,324 --> 00:08:37,899
July 2025.

217
00:08:38,303 --> 00:08:41,011
By October, India was still asking the US

218
00:08:41,024 --> 00:08:42,066
for more details.

219
00:08:43,345 --> 00:08:46,288
As of that reporting, there is no verdict.

220
00:08:47,704 --> 00:08:49,918
The trainers, the gunmen, the handlers in

221
00:08:49,918 --> 00:08:52,183
Karachi — everyone else in this story used

222
00:08:52,196 --> 00:08:53,316
violence directly.

223
00:08:54,546 --> 00:08:57,137
Rana handed over something quieter.

224
00:08:57,556 --> 00:08:58,519
A business card.

225
00:08:58,923 --> 00:08:59,626
An office.

226
00:09:00,030 --> 00:09:01,410
A room at the Taj.

227
00:09:02,583 --> 00:09:04,992
And behind all of it, a friendship from a

228
00:09:05,005 --> 00:09:07,870
boarding school in Punjab, still intact

229
00:09:07,883 --> 00:09:08,911
forty years later.

230
00:09:09,315 --> 00:09:11,528
An American jury decided that wasn't

231
00:09:11,542 --> 00:09:13,638
enough to make him part of the attack.

232
00:09:14,914 --> 00:09:17,050
Now an Indian court has to decide:

233
00:09:17,688 --> 00:09:19,289
was the cover story a weapon?
